0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","4.11",{"topic":{"id":"4.11","title":"Appreciation and Evaluation of Digital Evidence","minutes":12,"summary":"Evaluate the whole evidential record: reliability, relevance, corroboration, alternatives, and the burden of proof.","sections":[{"title":"Ask what the evidence proves","body":["For each item, identify the proposition it supports. A preserved email may prove that a message existed in a mailbox, while payment records may show a transfer. Neither automatically proves who controlled the account or intended the result. Build a chain of propositions and identify the weak links rather than treating “digital evidence” as one indivisible category.","Consider source reliability, acquisition integrity, interpretation, and context. A valid hash supports comparison; a competent custodian supports provenance; an application log may support an event. Different weaknesses require different remedies. More files do not necessarily mean stronger proof if they are duplicates or irrelevant."]},{"title":"Corroboration and contrary evidence","body":["Independent sources are more valuable than repeated copies of the same claim. Compare endpoint logs, provider records, bank references, and witness accounts while noting their different time bases and scopes. A contradiction may reveal a clock error, a missing event, a false statement, or an incorrect assumption.","Test the defense or alternative explanation fairly. Shared access, compromised credentials, scheduled activity, and business-process failure may explain an artifact. The burden of proof remains a legal question, not a threshold in a forensic tool. An examiner should not fill evidential gaps with personal confidence."]},{"title":"From technical finding to legal evaluation","body":["Admissibility, authenticity, and weight are distinct. A record may be admitted but carry little weight because its source is unclear. Another may be highly informative but require further proof of lawful collection or certification. The court evaluates the record in the context of the applicable law and the whole case.","Prepare a balanced evidence matrix with proposition, source, support, limitation, and corroboration. This helps counsel and investigators identify what remains to be proved. Avoid presenting a probability generated by a model as the probability that the accused committed the offence; the two questions are fundamentally different."]}],"practice":{"title":"Find the weakest link","scenario":"A case combines a matching hash, a shared-account login, and a bank transfer.","steps":["Write the proposition supported by each item.","Identify where account activity is being converted into human attribution.","Seek independent evidence for that link.","Explain why the hash does not cure the attribution gap."],"debrief":"Strong integrity at one stage cannot compensate for an unsupported inference at another. Evaluate the chain proposition by proposition."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["bsa","anvar","nist-guide"],"labId":"certificate","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain ask what the evidence proves and apply it to an investigation.","Explain corroboration and contrary evidence and apply it to an investigation.","Explain from technical finding to legal evaluation and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.11","prompt":"Why are independent corroborating sources valuable?","options":["They eliminate every possible error.","They can test the same proposition from different origins.","They always contain identical timestamps.","They make legal procedure unnecessary."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Independent sources can support or challenge a proposition more meaningfully than repeated copies of one record.","unit":4}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
